    },
    {
      "id": "kde1x5w",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<img></img>Siachen Glacier: Longest glacier in the Karakoram and 2nd longest non-polar glacier in the world (~76 km); highest battlefield in the world",
        "Baltoro Glacier: Karakoram; one of world's largest glaciers",
        "Biafo Glacier: Karakoram; ~67 km long",
        "Kolahoi Glacier: Source of Liddar river; Pahalgam area",
        "Zemu Glacier: Borders with Sikkim",
        "Drung-Drung Glacier: Zanskar region"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "i1gsj0f",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1.8 Climate"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "c6h26n2",
      "content": "J&amp;K has diverse climate due to varied altitudes:"
    },
    {
      "id": "yxzav1p",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Region</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Climate</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Jammu Region</strong></p>",
            "<p>Sub-tropical; hot summers (38–45°C), mild winters; low rainfall ~600–900 mm; monsoon impact</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Kashmir Valley</strong></p>",
            "<p>Temperate; mild summers (~30°C), cold winters (-5 to -15°C); snowfall Nov–March; 'Chillai Kalan' = 40 coldest days (Dec 21 – Jan 31)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Ladakh</strong></p>",
            "<p>Cold desert; extreme cold (-30 to -40°C in winter); low rainfall (&lt;100 mm/yr); rain shadow area</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "poeg8qt",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Chillai Kalan: 40 days (Dec 21 – Jan 31) — coldest period in Kashmir",
        "Chillai Khurd: 20 days (Feb 1 – Feb 20)",
        "Chillai Bachha: 10 days (Feb 20 – March 2)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "v4ts260",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1.9 Forests & Vegetation"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Focus on critical mountain passes connecting the Kashmir Valley, Jammu, and Ladakh regions.",
        "Master the origins, tributaries, and barrage/hydropower locations along the Jhelum and Indus river systems.",
        "Memorize major high-altitude lakes, prominent glaciers, and national parks for static GK questions."
      ],
      "id": "mmhiwia"
    },
    {
      "id": "2pirfz6",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Forest area: ~20,230 sq km (about 20% of J&amp;K); managed by J&amp;K Forest Department",
        "Types: Tropical thorn forests (Jammu), sub-tropical pine forests, temperate forests (deodar, fir, spruce), alpine meadows (margs), sub-alpine &amp; alpine scrub",
        "Kashmir Margs (high-altitude meadows): Gulmarg, Pahalgam, Sonamarg, Yusmarg, Doodhpathri",
        "Chinar (Platanus orientalis): State tree; extensively planted by Mughals in Kashmir",
        "Saffron: Pampore (world's largest saffron-growing region)",
        "Walnut &amp; Apple: Major horticulture products; Kashmir is India's largest apple-producing state"
      ]
    },
    {
